The system should not immediately invoke a frontier model for full re-exploration. It should follow an escalation gradient:

The key variable across all four phases is confidence. A workflow is a set of judgment assets with confidence scores, not rigid rules. Each successful execution raises confidence. Each failure lowers it. When confidence drops below a threshold, the system re-invokes model exploration.

Combining the four phases:

The model opens the road. The system paves it. [...] But expand one layer, and every step contains ifs:

`for email in this_week:
if is_spam(email):
skip(email)
if is_newsletter(email):
archive(email)
if is_work_related(email):
project = infer_project(email)
if project.confidence > 0.85:
classify_into_project(email, project)
else:
ask_user_or_create_candidate_project(email)
if contains_deadline(email):
date = extract_date(email)
if date.confidence > 0.9:
create_task(email, date)
else:
mark_for_review(email)` [...] Luo Fuli, who led DeepSeek-V2 research before joining Xiaomi’s MiMo team in late 2025, framed it in a recent interview as a product of three factors: effectiveness × cost × efficiency. The framing matters because it forces agent design back from the “fully autonomous” myth to the engineering reality of the runtime. None of the three is optional. Effectiveness without cost control cannot become a commercial product. Low cost without trustworthy results creates no willingness to pay. Efficiency
